{"text": "Commercial culture, generating the stories, images, and sounds that mattered most to the public, pushed folk culture practices underground. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> People still composed and sang songs and amateur writers still scribbled verse. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> At the same time, grassroots fan communities emerged in response to mass media content. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Some media scholars hold on to the useful distinction between mass culture a category of production and popular culture a category of consumption. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> They argue that popular culture is what happens to the materials of mass culture when they get into the hands of consumers, when a song played on the radio becomes so associated with a particularly romantic evening that two young lovers decide to call it our song, or when a fan becomes so fascinated with a particular television series that it inspires her to write original stories about its characters. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> In other words, popular culture is what happens as mass culture gets pulled back into folk culture. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> The culture industries never really had to confront the existence of this alternative cultural economy because, for the most part, it existed behind closed doors and its products circulated only among a small circle of friends and neighbors.", "alignment": {"characters": ["C", "o", "m", "m", "e", "r", "c", "i", "a", "l", " ", "c", "u", "l", "t", "u", "r", "e", ",", " ", "g", "e", "n", "e", "r", "a", "t", "i", "n", "g", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "s", "t", "o", "r", "i", "e", "s", ",", " ", "i", "m", "a", "g", "e", "s", ",", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "s", "o", "u", "n", "d", "s", " ", "t", "h", "a", "t", " ", "m", "a", "t", "t", "e", "r", "e", "d", " ", "m", "o", "s", "t", " ", "t", "o", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "p", "u", "b", "l", "i", "c", ",", " ", "p", "u", "s", "h", "e", "d", " ", "f", "o", "l", "k", " ", "c", "u", "l", "t", "u", "r", "e", " ", "p", "r", "a", "c", "t", "i